==========================================
DAY 07 NOTES — Logs & Log Analysis
Date: 03-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- Most logs live in /var/log/
- syslog = general system activity, auth.log = login/authentication events
- cat is fine for small logs, but tail/grep are needed for huge ones
- tail -f follows a log LIVE as new lines get written (great for watching in real time)
- grep -c counts matching lines instead of printing them all
- auth.log is the first place to check for brute-force login attempts
- Most log files need sudo to read (they contain sensitive info)
- Log files rotate — older ones might be named auth.log.1 or syslog.gz

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] ls /var/log
[ ] tail -20 / tail -f
[ ] grep "Failed password" / grep -c
[ ] grep + awk + sort + uniq combo for IP counting

fill this in after running commands yourself :
$ ls /var/log
> 

$ sudo tail -20 /var/log/syslog
> 

$ sudo grep -c "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log
> 


